6. MRM Assessment

Strengths

Regulatory relevance: Directly targets HFT-related latency abuse, aligning with MAR 1.6 and global market-conduct standards.

Quantitative clarity: The metric translates latency effects into USD Value, providing measurable and comparable outputs.

Cross-venue coverage: By analyzing instrument-specific activity (rather than single-exchange feeds), the model detects cross-exchange stuffing affecting consolidated tapes.

Robust architecture: Uses exchange-capacity and trader-rate benchmarks derived from empirical data, ensuring realism and defensibility.

Weaknesses / Limitations

Data intensity: Relies on high-resolution order-message data and accurate timestamps, which may vary across venues.

No profit verification: The model quantifies potential gain but does not confirm actual arbitrage profits.

Calibration sensitivity: Choice of thresholds for capacity outliers and latency impact can materially affect alert frequency.

Opaque parameters: Public exchange-capacity estimates may not fully reflect proprietary infrastructure or dynamic bandwidth changes.

Governance Recommendations

Perform back-testing using confirmed historical stuffing events to validate USD Value-score thresholds.

Establish data-quality checks for order-message completeness and synchronized time stamps.

Document parameter-calibration rationale (e.g., percentile cut-offs for capacity exceedance).

Reassess model sensitivity semi-annually as exchange technologies and HFT patterns evolve
